{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/266837-hunter-chronicle-fan-stop-gap-material","id":266837,"title":"Hunter Chronicle - fan stop-gap material","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-09-27T10:33:03-07:00","id":266837,"title":"Hunter Chronicle - fan stop-gap material","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Themes:</b><br /> <br /> Light in the darkness; a fire to cleanse and a light to remember.<br /> <br /> Humankind is both powerful and fragile. Fragile when we are alone and ignorant, but powerful when we gather our resources and<br /> find knowledge.<br /> <br /> <b>Moods:</b><br /> <br /> Horror and mystery.<br /> <br /> The two things that drive people to take up the Vigil: undeniable evil, and an irresistible puzzle.<br /> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:blue\">Inspired by <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/266374-sell-me-on-hunter-2nd-edition\">this thread.</a> Title says it all. Feel free to contribute anything you can!</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":877,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/877-mugasofer","user":"MugaSofer","date":". 09-27-2014, 02:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/877-mugasofer","name":"MugaSofer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar877_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-27T11:17:55-07:00","id":266882,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:blue\">First things first: the game mechanics:</span><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Compact Initiation (\u2022 to \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022)</b><br /> <br /> Every group that interacts with the supernatural, from the most informal group of friends to the most elaborate conspiracy, has two things: a hierarchy, and tools for dealing with the supernatural.<br /> <br /> Compact Initiation reflects membership in one of these esoteric groups. The dot rating dictates access. One dot is an associate of the group, two a respected member, three a field agent, four a specialist operative, five is a unique asset in their own right.<br /> <br /> If you wish your character to begin play with Compact Initiation, work with your Storyteller to determine the details of their organization. Most often, the player characters belong to or are allied with a particular group; examples will be given below. Alternatively, you may wish, during the course of play, to begin your own Compact based on what you've learned about the supernatural.<br /> <br /> Designing a new Compact requires three things, at bare minimum. First is a Purpose. This is the defining reason they exist; their &quot;mission&quot; and concept. Usually, it\u2019s tied in with the group\u2019s history and recent background.<br /> Second is a Doctrine. Every group of people is defined by its rules and traditions, explicit or otherwise. But more importantly, a Doctrine is a way of viewing the world: a framework for explaining and dealing with all the strange things they encounter.<br /> <br /> The last thing is a Power. This is a demonstration of their Doctrine that both grounds members\u2019 faith and helps them in their Purpose. This could be as &quot;mundane&quot; as some really powerful military hardware, reaffirming that Humanity has ways to deal with monsters, human or supernatural. To a religious Compact, it could be a miracle. To a scientific one, it might be a &quot;supernatural&quot; phenomenon pinned down and understood. Newly-created Compacts will not yet have an appropriate Power; their members also cannot purchase the higher levels of Compact Initiation.<br /> <br /> Compact Initiation can be used to provide bonuses or penalties to appropriate Social rolls, much like the Status Merit.<br /> A Compact Initiation Merit also offers specific benefits at each level of influence. When creating a Compact, you need to develop these as well. The following are basic guidelines:<br /> <br /> \u2022 A Skill Specialty or one-dot Merit.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022 A one-dot Merit.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 A Skill dot or a two-dot Merit (often a supernatural Merit).<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 A three-dot Merit, often supernatural in origin.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 A three-dot Merit or a major advantage not reflected in game traits. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":877,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/877-mugasofer","user":"MugaSofer","date":". 09-27-2014, 02:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/877-mugasofer","name":"MugaSofer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar877_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-27T12:30:13-07:00","id":266953,"title":"","content":"I'm going to focus on the core 12, because that's the bit I find most interesting. Here's how I'd polish them up.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#0000FF\">(One thing I want to make clear, these Themes and Moods are like all themes and moods, just guidelines not straight jackets). </span><br /> <br /> <u><b>Ashwood Abbey</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: Addicted to Novelty - an abbey game is about trying to find something to amuse the person who's already done everything. Have you hunted a werewolf? Well it would be boring to do that again, maybe this time you'll be hunted by a werewolf, can you survive long enough to reach the safe zone where the Abbey waits with silver? Done both, well now you have to try and hunt with a Werewolf. Then it's let a wolf spirit possess you before you hunt with werewolves. Eventually you have to switch to vampires because you've done all the simple werewolf stuff and only have suicidal stuff left.<br /> <br /> Mood: Forbidden Thrills - self explanatory.<br /> <br /> <br /> Firstly I'd focus more on the idea of it being a franchise. The Null Mysteriis ask how can a bunch of board spoilt rich brats be so successful at hunting? The answer is the staff. The clubhouses may look like they're staffed with stuffy British butlers, but they're actually staffed with extreemly well paid ex-military, ex-cons (the really good ones who know how to organise organised crime), and successful hunters.<br /> <br /> Sometimes they're the ones who capture monsters for the party. Sometimes they soften them up a little. Sometimes they just point the way to a monster and let the rich kids have their fun. But when you buy the Abbey's franchise, what you're really buying is the people who can recurite and run this top tier staff. (That and the Abbey's prestigious name)<br /> <br /> I'd also focus on money as much as guns. The Abbey is more likely to hire a band of changeling musicians for a concert than they are to hunt them. Of course they still hunt, but I'd play up the hiring<br /> <br /> New Material: A condition to enforce the theme.<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Long Night</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: Faith Makes the Man - The Faithful are made strong by faith. The Merciful are made kind by faith. The Hopeless escaped their past through faith. A Long Night game is about how faith makes you; and how faith or lack of it also made your enemies.<br /> <br /> Mood: Hope in the Darkest Hour.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't really change much with the long night, nor do I think they need much new material. (God himself is more of an MM thing. In the Long Night he's a distant deity you have faith in)<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Loyalists of Thule</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: Unearthed Secrets - The Loyalists of Thule aren't just about secrets. They're about secrets that change you, your shameful secret that you must atone for. But simpler ones, from discovering someone you trust sold her son to the Gentry, that changes your relationship with her permanently. All the way up to discovering a Leviathan's autobiography and going mad from the revelation.<br /> <br /> Mood: Bitter Resignation - The Loyalists don't want to be doing what they do. But for one reason or another they do it anyway, they don't fight their situation, but they sure don't like it.<br /> <br /> I'd give them more of an network. Not a true organisation but talk a bit about how they share knowledge.<br /> <br /> New Material: Nazis. Like the MM has Vampires and the Lucifuge has demons, Nazi's are the Loyalist's defining enemy. I'd do a little bit about completly mundane neo Nazi's. I'd do a little bit on the comparison between Nazi's and cults in general. And a bigger bit on what occult weaponry / mad science the Third Riceh has, and who might still have it. (There's bits of occult WWII around the books here and there, Moral Remains did it really well, but not really enough for a Storyteller to easily throw up some remanent of WWII as an antagonist for the Loyalists).<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Network Zero</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: The Truth is Out There - Net0 knows the truth is out there. Unlike other hunters they don't hide their belief, and they're ostracised for it. A Net0 game is about being ostracised and shunned for doing the right thing, for sticking to your principles. But it's also about how overcoming those barriers make victory so much sweeter. When everyone ignores your warning about the zombies you're all hiding with a horde banging at the doors, and you've done the research and can save everyone. When the men in black turn turn up and sheepishly apologise for censoring you because <i>they need your help</i>. It's about being the person no-one thought would amount to anything, and proving them all wrong.<br /> <br /> Mood: Isolation - see above.<br /> <br /> I don't think they need any new material.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b><u>Null Mysteriis</u></b><br /> <br /> Theme: Don't Underestimate Science - You live in a world where people don't believe in global warming, and hunters are the sort of people likely to buy into homeopathy. They go against the consensus and they're proud of it. Everyone looks for answers in dusty tomes of lore, or by interrogating a witch. It never occurs to them that science is a methodology. You can test things, record the results, refine your theories. It's such a simple idea, but even with the supernatural, it works. Or to put it another way. Net0 is about succeeding desptie adversity. A Null Msyeriis game is about succeeding because you're methological and rational.<br /> <br /> Mood: Patient.<br /> <br /> I think they need a bit of material defining the difference between scepticism (monsters don't exist) and scepticism (lets test this before we jump to conclusions); and that NM are the second kind - but other hunters sometimes don't realise the difference. The sidebar &quot;it's decision time&quot; also needs to go into the corebook.<br /> <br /> I'd also give some examples of experiments and breakthroughs players might come up with.<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>The Union</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: Light in Darkness - lets be honest, the Union was always an excuse to write a splat about the most typical motivation of all, and that's perfectly fine.<br /> <br /> Mood: Horror - but they aren't in it for the mysteries.<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Aegis Kai Doru</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: Ancient plans, modern nights. The AKD might look Greek, but they're might be older than the Ascending Ones, pre-dating the breaking of the world. What's more they know what happened, or at least they think they do. What's more they have a plan to fix it, because that's the core theme. Normally (not always, but normally) monsters act, hunter's react. A monster has a plan to eat the world, or your girlfirend, and you stop it. Not so with the AKD, with the AKD the whole point is that it's your plan that drives the story. You're not just hoarding relics, you're looking for specific ones and when you find them everything you lost in the breaking of the world will be yours again. The other relics are just useful.<br /> <br /> Mood: Epic power on an epic time frame. (Epic being in the Greek sense). In the AKD game things that happened in history should matter tonight. If a monster attacks once every hundred yeas, the AKD are the ones with a reminder in their calendar to stop it. The diary of an ancient AKD hunter is more than a guide through the labyrinth he built, it's emotionally resonant with the player's own vigil. And as for epic power, the AKD are in the big leagues, rivalling the Seers and the Pentacle. They exist in a cold war, sending pawns and proxies against each other, because both sides quite literally have magical nukes.<br /> <br /> <br /> Earlier on I suggested focusing the AKD on Mummies instead of Mages and Werewolves. I've changed my mind. The AKD shouldn't be focused on an ancient grudge, it detracts from their focus on their own epic plans. (Though Mummies do have lots of Relics to steal).<br /> <br /> So basically, like all Conspiracies I'd give them an actual organisation. I'd also give them a list of potential origins, each origin comes with it's own epic Plan. Though some are more epic than others. A guardian of the veil Laberynth or Mummy cult that went rogue, or barbarians who clubbed refugee Atlantians over the head and stole their stuff not epic. The priests of a benevolent deity cast down by the Exarchs, determined to free the deity so she can gather all the AKD, alive or dead, and then finally depart the universe for a less broken one - epic.<br /> <br /> It's up to the storyteller if their plan can ever succeed. And feel free to pick one as a true origin and another as what they believe.<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Ascending Ones</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: Beauty Broken Things. The Ascending Ones are broken. The Cult of the Phoenix was one half of a greater whole, and the Cult of Set was lost. The Knife of Paradise are not upstanding priests, but fanatics and fundamentalists, sometimes even outright terrorists. The Jagged Cresent, drug dealers. But they're also alchemists, alchemists know that nigredo comes before rubedo. The inner city drug dealer finds something worth protecting among the poverty. The fundamentalist is sincere in his love for god and desire to help his community - if he says homosexuality is a sin he genuinely wants nothing more than to protect the homosexual from hell, he's wrong (probably, it <i>is</i> the WoD) but means right. The Southern Temple sees beyond the loss of Ancient Egypt, and realises it's eternal in the memory of mankind.<br /> <br /> Mood: Sacrafice and Rebirth. The Ascending Ones are all about sacrifice, every Elixir is a sacrifice as drugs damage the mind and body. But the more they sacrifice the more they gain. Even if they die in battle, the Ascending One gains everything in the afterlife. Letting players monologue their entry to heaven might be appropriate.<br /> <br /> <br /> More than any other group, I think the Ascending Ones really need an explanation of how they operate, and one that actually makes sense (sorry Compacts and Conspiracies). They're an alliance of three different Conspiracies, and that really needs to be made clear for it to make sense.<br /> <br /> The gaps that need to be filled: Egyptian Gods, and Abrahamic God. Uniquely, advice on how to portray the relationship between them. Is Exodus correct to say that Abrahamic God can defeat the Egpytian Gods? Are they broadly equal? Does the difference in worshippers make a difference? Are the Egpytian Gods still worshipped in the WoD? Advice and options for all.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Cheiron Group</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: Bureaucracy Makes the Monster. In Cheiron the average hunter isn't evil. The average scientists dissecting a young changeling isn't completely evil. Even the board of directors isn't evil. It's the corporation itself. No, it's not alive (not unless you want to say it's inferstructure. The subverted version of Cherion is one big Inferstructure, with the board of directors as it's linchpin). Rather it's a lot of people who want to do good, feed their families, cure a disease, and the bureaucracy makes them all powerless to change it. The Bureaucracy offers carrots (cut this corner and you'll be able to invest in that rare disease) and sticks (cut this corner or you're fired). The humans go along or get fired, and their body is Cherion property.<br /> <br /> Mood: Detachment.<br /> <br /> <br /> The gaps that need to be filled: Just the board of directors, and the secret origin hinted at by the logo. With more wordcount more choices can be offered. My favourite is that they're a big cult worshipping Cheiron (the centaur). In exchange for supernatural skill at medicine they make sacrifices, hence all the studying supernatural to death, and sending cannon fodder out to die. Cheiron used to be religious, but now they think of it as a business partnership. Sacrifices in, rewards out. (I know that dosn't quite fit the theme, but that's why it's an option).<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Lucifuge</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: What makes a monster? Hunter has always talked about what really is the diving line between a monster and a hunter. The Lucifuge is the group built on that question.<br /> <br /> Mood: Introspective.<br /> <br /> <br /> First of all I want to make it so that the subgroups are 100% about a Lucifuge's self image. The Reconciliation isn't the group of fanatical monster hunters. It's the group who believe that all monsters, including themselves, can be redeemed. The Denial are the group who believe they're not monsters (I'm not a monster, I'm a hunter!) and the Truth are the group who're open minded and inquisitive about themselves. <br /> <br /> Secondly this Satan guy. Options needed. The guy from the bible, the guy from Dantae's Inferno, and of course more choices. Furthermore each option should come with a suggested effect on Castigation - and advice on what books to use to represent them. If they're all descended from biblical demons, then perhaps Castigations that say &quot;demons&quot; only work on the things from Inferno. If they're descended from any Ephemerial Being, and Castigations are rituals that anyone can use if they have the heritage to use Essence, perhaps it works on all ephemeral begins, or you choose when you take the ritual.<br /> <br /> Speaking of Castigation, unseen senses should come free at the first dot. I'm not saying they automatically know who's a demon and who's not, if a True Fae wants to pretend to be a demon, it's probably going to fool hunters. But I'm saying they should be pretty good at telling what's a demon (as defined by the storyteller's choice).<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Malleus Maleficarum</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: Patronisingly paternal. The MM come into your community, they take leadership, they protect you, they know best. The MM are the most mortal friendly Conspiracy (contrast to how they treat supernaturals), but they're undeniably self-righteous and blind. In God's Plan (if God exists), the MM's role is to protect humanity until they outgrow the need for those protectors and are ready to think for themselves. The horror is that maybe mortals never will.<br /> <br /> (Note - to make my personal atheistic point of view clear. I'm basing the MM on the medieval church, the one who said Bibles must be in Latin because otherwise the peasants might read them. Whatever you think about the modern church, I think we can all agree it's good that society outgrew that idea)<br /> <br /> Mood: Certainty.<br /> <br /> <br /> Gaps that need to be filled: Discussion on the Abrahamic god is needed. Is he what the MM believe in, the God of Abraham, Issac and Jacob but flawed when Christians believe him perfect, or something else entirely. Such as an Exarch or just a high ranked Spirit.<br /> <br /> <br /> <u><b>Task Force: Valkyrie</b></u><br /> <br /> Theme: Secrets and Trust. The MM are trying to teach their flock, but sheep don't learn. TF:V are trying to keep you ignorant, for your own good. And the thing is, it really is for your own good. These are WoD Mortals, they really would panic. What gives TF:V the right? A democratic election, and the president signed off for another year. Even their own members, they're given orders and not told why. It's called compartmentalization. Nobody spills the secrets, because nobody knows them all.<br /> <br /> But that's a lot of power. Sometimes it's abused. TF:V is about trust, but also the limits of trust. It's about power that can be used for good and bad, and the balance of oversight with efficiency (think secrets leaking, not red tape). Sometimes your questions reveals a corrupt officer, sometimes your questions just mean the answers find their way into a mind-reader's diary.<br /> <br /> Mood: Big guns. TF:V has power, more than any Conspiracy bar the AKD. Yes, they have actual nukes. A story about balancing power, and trusting or not trusting the people you vote to give power, well it requires power to be there in the first place. And sometimes you just want to attack vampires in helicopter gunships <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> Gaps that need to be filled: Not big guns actually. WoD Armrory did that nicely. Rather, just simple advice for how to run a major military operation. How do you storytell a combined Army, Navy and Airforce assult on Insmouth?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-27T14:22:37-07:00","id":267009,"title":"","content":"You can crib from <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/181307-compacts-conspiracies-as-mystery-cults\">this</a> if you'd like; is there any point in making Compact Initiation and Mystery Cult Initiation different merits?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have decided, after some thought, that I don't really feel happy on these forums. I might decide to come back to post. Who knows - but right now, I'm gone.<br /> <br /> So good bye, good luck, and have a nice day.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1170-amechra","name":"amechra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1170_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2316"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-27T14:53:45-07:00","id":267024,"title":"","content":"Well, for one thing, compacts aren't cults<br /> <br /> Which is why the &quot;often supernatural in origin&quot; should be taken out","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>&quot;Do the dead dare walk the Earth before my eyes? Will the undead raise an army, fall in, and advance? Will those who would abandon God and embrace the heretical order DARE to presume to meet my gaze!? Iscariot will not allow it. <b>I</b> will not allow it.&quot;</i>","edited":{"userid":3889,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3889-ninjasantaclause","user":"Ninjasantaclause","date":". 09-27-2014, 05:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3889-ninjasantaclause","name":"Ninjasantaclause","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-09-27T16:07:23-07:00","id":267039,"title":"","content":"Yeah, Compact Initiation <i>is</i> Mystery Cult Initiation. I just expanded the description a little to focus on tips for Hunter games. The mechanics are identical; I'm a huge fan of the Mystery Cult section of GMC.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/877-mugasofer","name":"MugaSofer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar877_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}